==English==

===Etymology===
First in use about 1735-40, from {{m|en|hum||(dialectal and slang) to delude, impose on, cajole}} + {{m|en|bug||a specter, goblin}}.

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|dated}} A [[hoax]], [[prank]] or [[jest]]
# {{context|dated}} A [[fraud]] or [[sham]]
# {{context|dated}} A [[fraudster]] or [[cheat]]
#* '''1903''', [[w:George Bernard Shaw|George Bernard Shaw]], ''[[w:Man and Superman|Man and Superman]]'', Act III:
#*: He means that he has sold out to the parliamentary '''humbugs''' and the bourgeoisie. Compromise! that is his faith.
# {{context|British|lang=en}} A type of [[chewy]] [[sweet]] ([[candy]])

